Quick diagnosis commands
Start by identifying what's causing the IO pressure:

bash
# See current IO wait and which processes are in D-state (uninterruptible sleep, usually waiting on IO)
top
# Press '1' to see per-CPU breakdown, look at %wa column

# Better real-time view of IO per process
iotop -o
# -o shows only processes actually doing IO

# Per-device IO stats
iostat -xz 2
# Look at %util (saturation), await (latency), r/s + w/s
The key metrics to watch in iostat:

%util near 100% = device is saturated
await high (>20ms for SSD, >50ms for HDD) = slow response
aqu-sz (avg queue size) high = requests piling up
Common Proxmox-specific causes
1. ZFS ARC or scrubs

If you're on ZFS, check for running scrubs:

bash
zpool status
zpool iostat -v 2
2. VM/LXC disk activity

Identify which guest is the culprit:

bash
# See IO per VM
pvesh get /cluster/resources --type vm --output-format json | jq '.[] | {vmid, name, diskread, diskwrite}'

# Or live per-VM IO
for vm in $(qm list | awk 'NR>1 {print $1}'); do
  echo "VM $vm:"
  cat /sys/fs/cgroup/qemu.slice/*qemu.scope/io.stat 2>/dev/null | grep $vm
done
3. Backup jobs

Check if vzdump is running — backups hammer IO:

bash
ps aux | grep -E 'vzdump|pbs'
journalctl -u pve-daily-update -n 50
4. Swap thrashing

bash
free -h
vmstat 2 5
# High 'si'/'so' columns = swap IO
5. Journald / log spam

bash
journalctl --disk-usage
du -sh /var/log/*
