Datacenter → [your node] → Summary, then switch the time range to "Hour (average)" — look at the IO delay graph to confirm the spike window.

Then check each VM/LXC: [VM/CT] → Summary → look at "IO delay" and "Disk IO" graphs around that 15-minute window. Whichever guest shows a matching spike is your culprit.

Check what was running at that time
bash
# Backup jobs in the last hour
journalctl --since "30 min ago" | grep -iE 'vzdump|backup'

# Any task that ran via Proxmox
cat /var/log/pve/tasks/index | tail -50
# Then inspect a specific task:
# cat /var/log/pve/tasks/<first-char>/<UPID>

# ZFS scrub/resilver activity
zpool history | tail -30
zpool status

# Cron jobs that fired
journalctl --since "30 min ago" | grep -i cron

# Kernel/IO errors (disk going bad?)
dmesg -T | tail -100
journalctl -k --since "30 min ago" | grep -iE 'error|blocked|hung|timeout'
The blocked for more than X seconds kernel messages are a big red flag — that's tasks stuck waiting on IO, often a sign of a failing disk or saturated storage.

Check per-VM historical IO
bash
# Pull RRD data for each VM around the spike (last hour)
for vmid in $(qm list | awk 'NR>1 {print $1}') $(pct list | awk 'NR>1 {print $1}'); do
  echo "=== VM/CT $vmid ==="
  pvesh get /nodes/$(hostname)/qemu/$vmid/rrddata --timeframe hour 2>/dev/null | \
    jq -r '.[] | select(.diskread > 0 or .diskwrite > 0) | "\(.time | todate) read=\(.diskread) write=\(.diskwrite)"' | tail -20
done
Do the same for LXC by swapping qemu for lxc in the URL.

Disk health — rule this out early
A single flaky disk can cause exactly this pattern (brief IO wall, then recovers):

bash
# SMART status on all disks
for d in /dev/sd? /dev/nvme?n?; do
  [ -e "$d" ] && echo "=== $d ===" && smartctl -H -A "$d" 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'health|reallocated|pending|crc|wear'
done

# ZFS pool errors
zpool status -v
Most likely suspects for a 10-minute spike
In order of probability on a home lab Proxmox:

Backup job (vzdump to your NAS) — very common, usually scheduled
LXC doing something heavy — your n8n workflows, FFmpeg assembly, or a container doing updates
ZFS scrub — runs monthly by default, can last much longer than 10 min but early phase could match
VM doing a snapshot or fstrim — weekly fstrim.timer is a classic
Failing disk — retries cause latency spikes
Check the fstrim timer specifically, since it's enabled by default and runs weekly:

