Find log volume spikes
The idea: bucket all journal entries by time and look for minutes with abnormally high message counts. A sudden burst of thousands of log lines often is the IO load, or points to whatever's thrashing.

bash
# Messages per minute across last 2 weeks — find the outlier minutes
journalctl --since "2 weeks ago" -o short-iso --no-pager | \
  awk '{print substr($1,1,16)}' | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30
The top entries are your spike windows. Note the timestamps — you'll grep around them next.

bash
# Same idea, per-hour buckets (easier to spot the pattern)
journalctl --since "2 weeks ago" -o short-iso --no-pager | \
  awk '{print substr($1,1,13)}' | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20
Find which service/unit is spamming
Once you have a suspicious timestamp (say 2026-04-22 14:30), zoom in:

bash
# What was logging the most during that minute
journalctl --since "2026-04-22 14:25" --until "2026-04-22 14:45" -o short-iso --no-pager | \
  awk '{print $4}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20

# Top systemd units in that window
journalctl --since "2026-04-22 14:25" --until "2026-04-22 14:45" \
  -o json --no-pager 2>/dev/null | \
  jq -r '._SYSTEMD_UNIT // "none"' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head
Error/warning storms specifically
bash
# Error-level and above, bucketed per hour
journalctl --since "2 weeks ago" -p err -o short-iso --no-pager | \
  awk '{print substr($1,1,13)}' | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20

# Most repeated error messages
journalctl --since "2 weeks ago" -p warning --no-pager | \
  awk '{$1=$2=$3=""; print}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20
That last one is gold — it collapses identical messages and shows you what's repeating. A hung NFS mount, a failing USB disk retrying, a cron loop, network errors retrying — all show up here.

Kernel ring buffer over time
Kernel IO/disk issues don't always hit journald cleanly:

bash
# All kernel messages in last 2 weeks, hourly buckets
journalctl -k --since "2 weeks ago" -o short-iso --no-pager | \
  awk '{print substr($1,1,13)}' | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20

# Anything scary
journalctl -k --since "2 weeks ago" --no-pager | \
  grep -iE 'blocked|hung|timeout|reset|error|fail|retry|i/o error|ata[0-9]|nvme|zfs|md/raid' | \
  awk '{$1=$2=$3=""; print}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30
Inside the containers/VMs too
The host journal only sees the host. If the IO comes from a guest, check each:

bash
# LXCs — check their logs too
for ct in $(pct list | awk 'NR>1 {print $1}'); do
  echo "=== CT $ct ==="
  pct exec $ct -- journalctl --since "2 weeks ago" -p warning -o short-iso --no-pager 2>/dev/null | \
    awk '{print substr($1,1,13)}' | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5
done
Your n8n container is a prime suspect here — if a workflow is retrying an HTTP call in a tight loop or an FFmpeg step is looping, it'll write logs like crazy.

If journald rotated the logs away
Two weeks is near the edge of default retention. Check:

bash
journalctl --disk-usage
journalctl --list-boots | head
If you only see a few days back, rotation ate the older ones. Check /var/log/ for archived files (*.gz, *.1, etc.) and your per-VM logs at /var/log/pve/ too.

