The normal systemd-logind heartbeat fires every 30 minutes (:01 and :31). Those two-at-a-time messages are fine.

The bursts at 23:37 and 01:43 are not the 30-minute timer — something is repeatedly triggering systemd-logind to attempt hibernation/suspend, getting blocked by kernel lockdown, and retrying in a tight loop. Each retry involves inspecting all session/user state, which means IO.

What's causing it
When a laptop lid closes, battery hits a threshold, or a power event fires, systemd-logind tries to execute its configured action (suspend/hibernate). Since your laptop is installed with Proxmox, the kernel is in lockdown mode (which blocks hibernation for security reasons), so the attempt fails. If the trigger keeps firing — bad battery reading, flapping AC adapter detection, lid switch bouncing — logind retries, and you get a storm.

The callbacks suppressed line at 01:45 confirms it: the kernel throttled the message rate because there were so many that it stopped printing them all. The real event rate during the spike was higher than the 10 visible lines.

Verify the trigger
Run these to confirm:

bash
