Why a permanently-closed lid still causes sporadic storms
When the lid is closed, the lid-switch sensor reports "closed" as a steady state. Logind only acts on state transitions (closed→open or open→closed), not on the steady state. So a closed lid sitting still = no events = silence.

The storms happen when the sensor briefly flips state and flips back — the kernel sees a fake "open" followed by "closed" (or vice versa), and each transition is a trigger. A flaky sensor doesn't misfire constantly; it misfires when something physically perturbs it.

What actually perturbs a closed lid sensor
Lid sensors on laptops are almost always Hall-effect sensors — a tiny magnet in the lid, a sensor on the base. They flip state based on magnetic field strength. Things that cause sporadic bouncing:

Temperature changes — the magnet's field weakens slightly as it heats up. A server under sustained load heats up, cools overnight, heats up again. Right at the threshold, the sensor oscillates.
Vibration — someone walking by, a fan vibration coupling through the chassis, a nearby door slam.
Power state transitions — USB device plug/unplug, display output changes, ACPI events can re-poll sensors and cause a brief re-read that falls on the wrong side of the threshold.
Aging/loose hinge cable — the flex cable running through the hinge degrades over time. Intermittent contact = intermittent readings.
Nearby magnetic fields — phone placed on top, a speaker, a hard drive nearby.
BIOS/firmware ACPI polling — some laptops re-poll lid state periodically, and a borderline reading flips.
Once you're near the Hall sensor's threshold, a tiny environmental change tips it. That's why you get days of silence and then a random 1-minute storm — the conditions only cross the threshold occasionally.

