Each pod that exposes metrics (your whole cluster)
On each scrape, Prometheus:

Opens HTTP connections to all targets
Receives the metrics bodies
Parses them
Appends to its TSDB on disk
The disk write part hits the worker node where Prometheus runs (worker2 in your case). It's write-heavy, not read-heavy, so it wouldn't match the read burst we saw.

But scrape fan-out CAN cause spikes on the apiserver side — every scrape of kube-state-metrics triggers LIST operations against apiserver, which reads from etcd. That matches the pattern.

Your real question — why not constant spikes if it scrapes constantly?
Here's why regular scrape activity doesn't cause 54% PSI spikes:

1. Scrapes have stable, predictable cost. Every 30 seconds, Prometheus does the same thing. The system is sized to handle that baseline. 15-30 MB/s of steady read activity is easy for an NVMe — your graphs show baseline is tiny (~1-3% PSI most of the time).

2. Spikes require something above baseline. You don't get 54% just from normal scraping. You get it when normal load combines with something unusual.

3. Prometheus is normally staggered. kube-state-metrics scrape might be at :00, :30. node-exporter scrapes at :10, :40. Different targets don't usually synchronize unless something specifically re-aligns them.

When Prometheus WOULD cause a spike
There are specific scenarios where scraping misaligns and stampedes:

A. Prometheus itself restarts. When it comes up, it scrapes all targets immediately to get a fresh baseline, rather than on its staggered schedule. This is an "all targets simultaneously" event. Check:

bash
kubectl -n monitoring logs prometheus-kube-prometheus-stack-prometheus-0 -c prometheus | \
  grep -E "starting|Start listening" | tail
If Prometheus restarted right before a spike, bingo.

B. Apiserver was briefly unavailable, then recovers. If apiserver had connectivity issues for 30 seconds, Prometheus builds up a queue of failed scrapes for kube-state-metrics and apiserver /metrics. When apiserver comes back, Prometheus retries everything at once. This is the "catch-up storm."

C. Scrape timeout + retry for kube-state-metrics. KSM is expensive. If a scrape takes >30s, it times out, and the next interval starts a new one. Under load, multiple concurrent scrapes against the same endpoint can pile up.

D. ServiceMonitor changes. If Flux reconciles a ServiceMonitor, Prometheus re-discovers targets and scrapes them fresh.

Key distinction: cause vs amplifier
I think Prometheus is probably an amplifier, not a cause, in your spikes:

Something primary happens (master1 hung, sssd blipped, cascade, etc.)
